// Types are decorators.
//
// Jade infers types, so a program names one only when it means to state what a
// variable or field holds. It does that with a decorator, which sits above the
// declaration. A decorator on a `let` is a call: `@int let n = v` is
// `let n = int(v)`.
//
// - On a built-in type, the decorator checks and never converts: int() returns
//   its argument unchanged when it is an int and refuses anything else, which
//   type_decorator_error.jde shows. An int is also a float, so
//   `@float let ratio = 1` holds 1.0. To convert, use a converter such as
//   toString, which works as a decorator the same way.
// - On a class, it declares the variable's class, which may be a parent of the
//   value's own: `@Animal let pet = Dog { … }`. That is `Animal(Dog { … })`,
//   a class view, which class_view_error.jde shows refused for a class the value
//   does not descend from.
// - On a field, it gives the field a type in place of a default, or checks the
//   default it has.

@int
let n = 42
print(n + 1)

@float
let ratio = 1
print(ratio)

@toString
let label = 7
print(label + "!")

class Animal {
    @public name
    @public fn speak() { return "..." }
}

class Dog(Animal) {
    @public fn speak() { return "woof" }
}

class Cat(Animal) {
    @public fn speak() { return "meow" }
}

// Declared as an Animal, so it may later hold a Cat.
@Animal
let pet = Dog { name: "rex" }
print(pet.speak())
pet = Cat { name: "tom" }
print(pet.speak())

// A field with a type and no default. A literal must give it a value of that
// type, and code that reads it knows what it is.
class Kennel {
    @public @int capacity
    @public @Animal resident
}

let k = Kennel { capacity: 2, resident: Dog { name: "ace" } }
print(k.capacity * 10)
print(k.resident.speak())
